<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haitian Vodou</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vodou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haitian Vodou is an Afro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> religion that developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> among enslaved Africans and their descendants, combining West and Central African traditions with elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. It incorporates spirits and practices with roots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> and Fon religion. Its Yoruba elements are cited as part of the cultural legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> in the Atlantic world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou.jpg" alt="Haitian Vodou" width="320" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sam Fentress · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VoodooValris.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of them captives of Dahomean wars, left the Bight of Benin through Ouidah and neighbouring ports, and their religious traditions shaped the Americas: the vodun of the Fon and their neighbours is a principal root of <b>Haitian Vodou</b> and of the Tambor de Mina tradition of northern Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Their religious practices became foundational to Candomblé in Brazil and Santería (Lucumí) in Cuba, while Yoruba, or Nago, elements also entered <b>Haitian Vodou</b> alongside its predominantly Fon and Kongo roots.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Nearly all speak Haitian Creole, French the educated 42%; 55% were Catholic and 29% Protestant in 2018, while 50–80% keep some element of <b>Vodou</b>, recognised by the state in 2003.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>This produced a broad free peasantry, unusual in the plantation Americas, but it ended large-scale sugar exports, reduced state revenue and left a gulf between the rural majority, who spoke Haitian Creole and practised <b>Vodou</b> alongside Catholicism, and a French-speaking urban elite of officials, merchants and landowners.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vodou</b>, blending Catholic ritual with the religions of Guinea, Congo and Dahomey, was outlawed and driven underground, but healed and held them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They built lives of their own as far as they could: families, the Creole language, and <b>Vodou</b>, which joined African religions with Catholic rites.</blockquote><a href="/societies/saint-domingue">Social Hierarchy of Saint-Domingue</a></li></ul></section>
